    "body": "Tesla has unveiled Powercore, a solid-state medium-voltage transformer that also handles the inverter and breaker functions in a single unit. Tesla rates it at 7.4 MW (9 MVA) continuously at 34.5 kV, with grid-forming support up to 10 MVA of peak power.\nIt’s made in America and scheduled to start operating in 2027. That’s the same year Heron Power, the startup founded by Tesla’s former top engineer Drew Baglino, plans to start volume production of its own solid-state transformer, the Heron Link.\nUpdate: Tesla has since released a full spec sheet for Powercore, including efficiency, warranty, and its data center plans. We’ve updated the article with the new details.\nTesla’s transformer plan finally gets a name\nTesla first said it would build its own transformers in September 2025, when it unveiled Megapack 3 and Megablock . Megablock connects four Megapack 3 units to a transformer and switchgear, but Tesla shared next to nothing about the transformer itself.\nAdvertisement - scroll for more content\nPowercore is that missing piece. Tesla announced it today through its Tesla Megapack account on X:\nIntroducing Powercore Solid state medium voltage transformer, inverter and breaker – reliable, safe and secure 7.4 MW/9 MVA continuously at 34.5 kVac w/ grid-forming support for 10MVA of peak power Made in America. Operating 2027 pic.twitter.com/sHxqnpWJrf — Tesla Megapack (@Tesla_Megapack) November 17, 2026\nAbout an hour later, Tesla followed up with a much longer post on X laying out the full spec sheet:\nPower: up to 9 MVA (7.4 MW) continuously at 34.5 kV AC\nPeak power: 10 MVA for grid-forming\nAC range: 10 kV to 34.5 kV\nDC range: 800 V to 2,000 V\nPeak efficiency: 98.86%\nAvailability: system guarantees of 99.9% and up\nWarranty: up to 25 years\nOperating range: -40°C to 55°C\nIt also packs a built-in solid-state medium-voltage breaker that Tesla says clears faults up to 1,000x faster than a conventional breaker. There’s no flammable oil, and it’s modular, so components can be serviced in the field without swapping the whole unit.\nTesla is pitching one product for three markets: battery storage, utility-scale solar, and 800 Vdc data centers. For Megablock, Powercore replaces the current two-step setup, where Megapack’s inverters output 480 V AC before a separate transformer steps it up to medium voltage. Tesla says that old architecture had twice the losses. For solar, Powercore works as a central inverter for today’s 1,500 Vdc arrays and upcoming 2,000 Vdc systems.\nTesla still hasn’t shared pricing, customers, or a factory location. It only says Powercore will be made in the US “of U.S. and imported parts” with first units operating in 2027.\nThe idea behind a solid-state transformer is simple enough. A typical solar or battery project uses low-voltage inverters to convert DC into AC, then a big oil-filled, iron-core transformer to step that up to grid voltage. A solid-state unit does both jobs with silicon carbide power electronics, which means less gear, a smaller footprint, and active control over voltage and frequency. That control is what “grid-forming” means: the unit can help hold the grid steady instead of just following it.\nAnd with lead times on conventional transformers stretching into years as data centers and renewable projects pile into interconnection queues, there’s real demand for an alternative.\nHeron had a head start\nYou can’t look at Powercore without thinking of Heron Power.\nBaglino spent 18 years at Tesla and finished as SVP of powertrain and energy, in charge of batteries, motors, power electronics, and energy products. He left in April 2024 and launched Heron Power in 2025 to build solid-state transformers.\nHe brought a lot of Tesla’s power electronics talent with him. As we reported when Tesla first announced its transformer plans , Heron hired engineers who led power electronics design for Powerwall 3, the next-gen Megapack, and Superchargers, along with the person who ran Tesla’s power electronics controls team for charging and energy products.\nSince then, Heron has raised a $38 million Series A, a $140 million Series B led by Andreessen Horowitz and Breakthrough Energy Ventures, and a $60 million credit facility from J.P. Morgan and TriplePoint Capital. Former Tesla CFO Zach Kirkhorn sits on its board, and Tesla co-founder JB Straubel is an investor.\nIt’s also building Heron Factory One, a 286,000-square-foot plant in Morgan Hill, California, designed to make 10,000 Heron Links a year. That’s over 40 GW of annual capacity, with commercial production planned for late 2027.\nAnd the customers are lining up. Vantage reserved more than 1 GW for its data centers, Intersect reserved 1.5 GW, LG Energy Solution Vertech is integrating Heron Link with its US-made battery systems, and RWE signed a pilot to install Heron Links at a Texas battery storage site in April 2027. Baglino says Heron has 50 GW of orders in the pipeline.\nPowercore vs. Heron Link\nHere’s how the two compare based on what each company has disclosed:\n | Tesla Powercore | Heron Link\nFunctions | MV transformer, inverter, breaker | Replaces LV inverter and MV transformer\nContinuous rating | 7.4 MW / 9 MVA | ~5 MW (solar/storage), ~4.2 MW (data center)\nPeak | 10 MVA (grid-forming) | Not disclosed\nAC voltage | 10 kV to 34.5 kV | Up to 34.5 kV\nDC voltage | 800 V to 2,000 V | 600 V to 800 V\nGrid-forming | Yes | Yes\nEfficiency | 98.86% peak | 98.6% DC-to-MV; over 98.5% MV-to-800 VDC\nAvailability | 99.9%+ system guarantee; up to 99.7% per unit (data centers) | 99.5% lifetime availability\nWarranty | Up to 25 years | Not disclosed\nData centers | MV straight to 800 Vdc, working with Nvidia | 34.5 kV AC to 800 VDC with integrated storage\nManufacturing | US, factory not disclosed | Morgan Hill, CA, 40 GW/year planned\nTimeline | First units operating 2027 | Pilot April 2027, volume late 2027\nAnnounced customers | None | Vantage, Intersect, RWE, LG ES Vertech\nOn paper, Powercore is the bigger box, and now it has the better numbers too. At 7.4 MW continuous, it carries about 50% more power per unit than the 5 MW Heron Link, and its 98.86% peak efficiency edges out Heron’s 98.6%. But Tesla’s figure is a peak number, and Heron doesn’t label its own as one, so it’s not quite apples to apples. Neither company has shared pricing.\nTesla is also going straight at Heron’s data center pitch. Powercore converts medium voltage directly to 800 Vdc, the rack architecture Nvidia is pushing, and Tesla says it’s working with Nvidia on 800 Vdc designs. Heron’s data center version does the same 34.5 kV AC to 800 VDC conversion with built-in storage, and data centers make up about a third of its business. Tesla’s answer to the storage piece is Megapack on the DC bus, and it says it already has 10 GWh of Megapack running at data centers.\nElectrek’s Take\nThis one is a little awkward for Tesla.\nA big chunk of the people who designed Tesla’s power electronics over the last decade now work at Heron, and the guy who ran the whole org is its CEO. Tesla’s energy business also lost its longtime leader, Mike Snyder, last month, according to the WSJ.\nTo be fair, Tesla still has plenty of power electronics expertise. Its silicon carbide inverters are in millions of cars and every Megapack. And it has something Heron doesn’t: a massive installed base and a sales channel. Tesla says it’s almost sold out of Megapacks for 2027, and bundling Powercore into Megablock is an easy upsell. Plenty of developers will take the one-stop shop.\nTesla’s specs now match or beat Heron’s on paper. But Heron has named customers, a pilot site with an install date, and a factory under construction. Tesla has none of those yet. I wouldn’t be surprised if Heron is actually ahead here.\nBut either way, the transformer shortage is big enough for both companies to sell everything they can build.\nAs data centers and new solar and battery projects pile onto the grid, utilities are scrambling for equipment and passing those costs on to ratepayers. With electricity rates up almost 10% last year and expected to keep climbing, going solar is one of the best ways to protect yourself against rising costs. And with lease and PPA options, you can do it with zero upfront cost and start saving immediately. If you want to find the best deal, check out EnergySage . It’s a free service with hundreds of pre-vetted installers competing for your business, so you save 20 to 30% compared to going it alone. No sales calls until you pick an installer. Get your free quotes here .\nFTC: We use income earning auto affiliate links. More.\nStay up to date with the latest content by subscribing to Electrek on Google News . You’re reading Electrek— experts who break news about Tesla , electric vehicles, and green energy , day after day. Be sure to check out our homepage for all the latest news, and follow Electrek on X , Facebook , and LinkedIn to stay in the loop. Don’t know where to start? Check out our YouTube channel for the latest reviews.",
